      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Cardiopulmonary bypass is known to affect cytokine release leading to a generalized endogenous immune reaction similar to that described in sepsis, without having been explored in great detail. Therefore we evaluated the anti- and pro-inflammatory cytokine responses after heart surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "16 patients who underwent coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery with extracorporeal circulation were included. ST2, IL-4 and IL-10 served as markers for TH2 cytokine response; IL-6, IL-8 and IFN-gamma as TH1 markers. Furthermore, total immunoglobulin subtype analysis (IgM, IgG, IgE) was performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Serum levels of soluble ST2 started to climb at 60 minutes (from 38 +/- 14 preoperatively to 1 480 +/- 890 pg/ml) and peaked 24 hours after surgery (13 360 +/- 2 840 pg/ml, P < 0.001). IL-10 reached a maximum at 60 minutes and returned to baseline levels 24 hours later. IL-6 and IL-8 levels peaked 60 minutes after surgery. IL-4 and IFN-gamma did not change. Only IgM showed a significant peak on day eight ( P < 0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Our results demonstrate that CABG surgery induces a massive long-lasting secretion of ST2, a protein related to immune suppression."
        }
      ]},
